  "text": "2012B13 Using an example of\nexponential decay, define and outline the\nimportant features of an exponential function.\nList:\n·\nExponential function\n·\nNatural exponential function\n·\nExponential decay\nExponential\nfunction:\nEquation\n·\ny = a\nx\n·\nOutput equals a constant to the power of the\ninput\nGraph\nRunaway curve\nNatural exponential\nfunction:\nEquation\n·\ny=Ae\nx\n=\nδ\ny/\nδ\nx (where e =\n2.718281828…)\n·\ni.e. rate of change of Y is proportional to\namount of Y\nGraphs\nUse\nUsed to model time-dependent exponential\nprocesses\n·\nDrug metabolism\n·\nAlveolar filling\n·\nPre-oxygenation\n·\nRadioactive decay\nExponential decay:\nEquation\nN = N\n0\ne\n-kt\nWhere\n·\nN = amount of isotope\n·\nN\n0\n= amount of isotope at\nt=0\nGraph\nRate constant (k)\n·\nProportion of isotopes decaying per unit time,\nhad the initial rate of decay continued\n·\nUnits: min\n-1\nTime constant (\nτ\n)\n·\nTime taken for completion had the initial rate\nof decay continued\n·\nτ\n= 1/k\n·\nUnits: minutes\n·\n~95% completion at 3 x\nτ\nHalf life (t\n1/2\n)\n·\nTime taken for 50% decay\n·\nt\n1/2\n=\nτ\nx\nlog\ne\n2\n·\n~97% completion =  5 x\nt\n1/2\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
